WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:01.612
DNSSEC has two halves.

2
00:00:01.612 --> 00:00:03.401
BIND validates by default.

3
00:00:03.401 --> 00:00:07.029
But behind the VPC Resolver,
BIND can't validate:

4
00:00:07.029 --> 00:00:12.613
the VPC Resolver ignores the DNSSEC
OK bit and returns no DNSSEC records.

5
00:00:12.613 --> 00:00:14.934
So validate in the VPC Resolver,

6
00:00:14.934 --> 00:00:18.951
or in BIND toward an upstream
that returns DNSSEC data.

7
00:00:18.951 --> 00:00:21.309
Oracle's VCN resolver validates,

8
00:00:21.309 --> 00:00:26.288
but whether it passes that data back
to BIND isn't documented; confirm.

9
00:00:26.288 --> 00:00:30.436
Private zones under a signed parent
go on BIND's exceptions list,

10
00:00:30.436 --> 00:00:32.001
held in the policy plane.

11
00:00:32.001 --> 00:00:35.431
And whether Protective
DNS returns DNSSEC records

12
00:00:35.431 --> 00:00:38.463
to forwarders is a point
to confirm with CISA.

13
00:00:38.463 --> 00:00:41.350
Signing: no cloud signs private zones.

14
00:00:41.350 --> 00:00:46.040
NIST says signing internal zones is
not recommended except where required,

15
00:00:46.040 --> 00:00:48.125
and notes agencies may require it.

16
00:00:48.125 --> 00:00:52.536
The design guidance is that the
zone's one writable owner signs it,

17
00:00:52.536 --> 00:00:57.650
with keys on the Grid Master or an HSM,
so a cloud BIND secondary holds no keys.
